From 849c592f7f695ca1cb2b67c18760d82863ad3806 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edward Wiles <emmabenzvi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 02:54:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] Part II.3: matched short real KNITRO trajectories,
 Reference vs C+

Two matched runs from chain1's own unperturbed calibration start (same
problem instance the real campaign used for delta=0.1), same wall
budget, differing only in cm_gradient_backend:

- 100s budget: both backends reach only n_eval=3/n_grad=1 (D=20/
  W=80000's own inner-solve cost dominates wall time before a second
  gradient call happens) -- bit-identical eval sequence between the two
  runs up through that single shared gradient use, confirming
  swapping backends produces byte-identical downstream KNITRO decisions
  when the gradient values agree (as II.1/II.2 already established).
- 600s budget: trajectories genuinely diverge (Reference: 11 iters/12
  grad evals; C+: 2 iters/3 grad evals) -- expected once more than one
  gradient call occurs, since even ~1e-13 gradient differences can feed
  KNITRO's own quasi-Newton curvature estimate differently. No evidence
  of incumbent-quality degradation: C+'s best cold-verified incumbent
  (gp=0.9837, independently re-verified to 8e-17) is at least as good as
  Reference's (gp=0.9801, re-verified to 2e-16) in this one-run
  comparison -- reported as a single n=1 data point, not overclaimed as
  "C+ is better."

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
